an). All required.\n\nEdge cases: What if a signature appears multiple times? We update with newest trial, i.e., overwrite last_trial, reflection, success with latest trial values. So dedup by signature: we keep only the latest trial for each signature.\n\nBut the instruction says \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" That supports overwriting.\n\nAlso note: The candidate_artifact says \"Feedback_provenance\", \"memory_bound\", \"stable_order\", \"no_weight_update\" are hard contracts. Probably they are just constraints: we must use the capacity from case['capacity'] as memory bound; stable order means we need to maintain deterministic order; no weight update means not modifying model; feedback_provenance likely means we should not fabricate reflections.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n1. Extract capacity = case['capacity'] (integer).\n2. trials = case['trials'] (list of dicts with signature, reflection, success).\n3. Initialize empty dict memory_map = {}.\n4. For i, trial in enumerate(trials):\n   sig = trial['signature']\n   memory_map[sig] = {\n       'last_trial': i,\n       'reflection': trial['reflection'],\n       'signature': sig,\n       'success': trial['success']\n   }\n   # This overwrites with latest trial.\n\n5. After loop, extract list of entries from memory_map.values().\n\n6. If len(entries) > capacity:\n   Sort entries by:\n       key = (success, -last_trial, signature) where success: False=0, True=1 -> so failures (False) sort before successes (True). For recency: we want newer (higher last_trial) to be kept, so sort by -last_trial. Then signature for tie-breaking.\n   Then entries = entries[:capacity].\n\n7. Then sort entries by last_trial ascending (chronological order).\n\n8. Build signatures list = [entry['signature'] for entry in entries].\n\n9. Return {\n       'memory': entries,\n       'signatures': signatures\n   }\n\nCheck examples? Not given expected outputs, but we can test with the provided cases.\n\nFirst 